# Appliances, vacuums and outdoor devices

Show whether a job is running, what the device reports and which commands are really available. Appliance integrations differ widely. A power sensor is not a washing-machine controller, and one robot's commands do not tell you another robot's.

This page is contract HA-CTRL-007. Its examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-007-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Information the integration supplies | What to show |
| --- | --- |
| Appliance cycle or progress | Only the phases and progress it reports. Label an estimate as an estimate. Keep a missing duration unknown. |
| Remote-control permission | Explain a known restriction beside the control. The interface cannot switch on a physical remote mode unless the integration supports that. |
| Vacuum features | Gate Start, Pause, Stop, Return and other commands one by one. Keep native maps or room IDs where supported. |
| Lawn-mower features | Keep mowing, paused, returning, docked and fault states separate. Do not reuse a vacuum's command list. |
| Remote entity | Sending a command does not prove the appliance received it or reports its state. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-007-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Off, idle, running, paused, returning and docked mean different things. A docked robot is not necessarily fully charged. A countdown reaching zero in the browser does not confirm the cycle ended. If the source gives no duration while paused, write “Not provided”, not a zero-minute finish.

Keep power, remote permission and fault apart. If a washer is off and remote control is off, do not show Start just because a generic service exists. Keep a fault code exactly as reported. Do not turn an unknown code into a confident explanation, and do not make an automatic restart the default fix.

If a request times out, keep the last reported job state and say the command may still complete. A room or task selection can change between review and submission, so recheck the supported targets. Never select every room silently.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-007-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use labels that name the device, such as “Return hall vacuum to dock”, and only when the device supports the command. Say what “Stop” does for that integration: pause, end or abort the cycle. Keep estimates visibly qualified. Do not infer completion, cleanliness, that nobody is home or that outdoor conditions are safe from a progress percentage or an icon.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-007-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Name the quantity a progress bar shows and give its value as text. Use an indeterminate state when there is no percentage. Do not announce every percentage point. Keep fault details and the native control reachable by keyboard, and name every icon-only control. Check long phase names and unknown estimates at narrow widths. Keep any useful manufacturer or map content as it is and do not recolour it.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-007-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Prefer the integration's native entity controls and supported card features. Capability can be split across sensors, switches and buttons, so write down the real mapping before designing a combined card. These examples do not add vacuum commands and do not extend the three household-card presentation adapters listed in the [compatibility register](compatibility-register.html). Do not infer a command from a manufacturer's name or a card that looks similar.

